<b>א.</b> "As a lion" - see Halakhot Ketanot, vol. 2, siman 3, and Sefer Bnei Chiyya.<br>
<br>
<b>ב.</b> "The dawn" - the Shelah writes of the sod to join day and night through Torah or prayer, morning and evening. As soon as one awakens he should wash his hands, and by this he is protected from wasted seed; certainly if he remains lying awake. Sefer Tola'at Yaakov cites the Zohar: one who walks four amot in the morning without handwashing is liable to death. Bach wondered why Beit Yosef omitted this. It appears because most cannot reliably observe it, and better they be unwitting than deliberate sinners; therefore it was not written (Shiyurei Knesset HaGedolah, and others).<br>
<br>
<b>ג.</b> "I place Hashem" - one should constantly visualize the Divine Name before his eyes (as written), the secret of "I have set Hashem before me always." This greatly benefits yirat shamayim (Ari z"l).<br>
<br>
<b>ד.</b> "The mockers" - in any case, one should not quarrel with them (Beit Yosef).<br>
<br>
<b>ה.</b> "With alacrity" - not literally instantly; one should pause a little and not stand abruptly (Gittin 70a). Seder Hayom writes that upon rising one says "Modeh Ani..." without needing netilat yadayim, even with unclean hands, since no Divine Name is mentioned (though Torah study before washing remains forbidden). After washing, one says the 42-letter Name text, "Ana beKoach."
